        "id": 1002270
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-09-20T15:51:19Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Sorry, I should have seen this before but I\u0027m uncomfortable with this. Someone can echo a message right back and get it to decrypt correctly. Could you add a parameter to |derive_encryption_key| that\u0027s just a 0 or 1 that indicates whether the message is from the EC or to it? That 0/1 would be an additional byte of input to HMAC-SHA256 and thus make the derived keys different in these two cases. Then a message encrypted *by* the EC cannot be echoed right back to it and be valid.",
      "revId": "e108558174a1ad80d9d281bc0f979bccfc039b45",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1e8d9506_f5f7b5a3",
        "filename": "common/fpsensor.c",
        "patchSetId": 13
      },
      "lineNbr": 658,
      "author": {
        "id": 1158184
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-09-20T16:01:03Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "That\u0027s by design, the EC is only decrypting the messages that it has encrypted itself. Its not exchanging messages with someone else, it\u0027s securing data that only itself can decipher later. So messages encrypted by the EC will be deciphered by the EC too, so the derived keys have to be identical. For more context, see the thread on crypto-help (sending link offline)",
      "parentUuid": "b43f6634_e089b73d",
      "revId": "e108558174a1ad80d9d281bc0f979bccfc039b45",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
